  "method_card": {
    "steps": [
      "Multiplier: increase is 1 + p/100, decrease is 1 − p/100.",
      "One change: multiply once. Compound: multiply by the multiplier to the power n.",
      "Reverse percentage: divide by the multiplier.",
      "Percentage change = (new − old) ÷ old × 100."
    ],
    "title": "Percentages & Compound Change",
    "content": "<p>Every percentage change uses a <strong>multiplier</strong>: a 20% rise is × 1.2, a 15% fall is × 0.85.</p><p><strong>Compound</strong> change applies the multiplier once per year, so after \\(n\\) years amount = start \\(\\times\\) multiplier\\(^n\\). This covers interest, depreciation and population change.</p><p>To find the <strong>original</strong> before a change (reverse percentage), divide by the multiplier instead of multiplying.</p>",
    "example": "<p><strong>£5000 at 3% compound interest for 4 years.</strong></p><p>Multiplier = 1.03. Value = 5000 × 1.03⁴ = 5000 × 1.12550881 = £5627.54 (2 d.p.)</p>"
  },
  "tier_guides": {
    "gold": {
      "steps": [
        "<strong>Combine changes</strong> by multiplying the multipliers: a 10% rise then a 10% fall is 1.1 × 0.9 = 0.99, not back to the start.",
        "<strong>Reverse a compound change</strong> by dividing by the combined multiplier.",
        "For 'how many years until', apply the multiplier year by year and count when the total passes the target."
      ],
      "title": "Gold: multi-step and problem-solving percentages",
